Below is a “thinking plan” that shows  

1. Which columns are likely to be useful for the four requested over‑take categories and why/when they would be filtered, and  
2. Ten progressively more complex exploratory SQLite queries (all with LIMIT 20) that you could run in a notebook or SQL‐client to understand the raw data that will finally allow you to count  

• Retirements (driver leaves the race)  
• Pit‑stop position changes (driver pits in laps 1‑5)  
• Start‑related changes (grid vs. lap‑1)  
• Standard on‑track passes (all other position swaps in laps 1‑5)  

────────────────────────────────────────
A. Relevant columns / potential usage
────────────────────────────────────────
lap_positions  
• race_id – scope to one race.  
• driver_id – who we are tracking.  
• lap – to restrict to laps 0‑5 (lap 0 = grid in this data‑set).  
• position – rank at that lap.  
• lap_type – ‘Race’, ‘Starting Position – Grid Drop’, … (filter only ‘Race’ once lap>0).  

pit_stops  
• race_id / driver_id – join key to lap_positions.  
• lap – < = 5 ⇒ early‑stop.  
• stop – we only care that at least one stop occurred.  

retirements  
• race_id / driver_id – join key.  
• lap – <= 5 means a retirement that can trigger a position gain for others.  
• status_id / retirement_type – optional extra info.  

results  
• race_id / driver_id – join key.  
• grid – official grid slot; used for “start‑related overtakes”.  
  (Alternatively lap_positions where lap=0, lap_type like '%Start%').  

lap_times / lap_times_ext (optional)  
• fastest_lap_time, etc. – not needed to count overtakes, but can be handy to confirm “out‑lap” after a pit stop when refining the logic.  

Typical conditions you will reuse  
• WHERE lap BETWEEN 1 AND 5               -- first five racing laps  
• WHERE lap = 0                           -- grid / start reference  
• WHERE "lap_type" = 'Race'               -- ignore formation / pit etc.  
• WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM pit_stops …) -- driver pitted that lap  
• WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM …)       -- driver did NOT pit nor retire  
• WHERE r."lap" <= 5                      -- early retirements only  

────────────────────────────────────────
B. Exploratory / building‑block queries (limit 20)
────────────────────────────────────────
```sql
-- 1) Distinct races that have lap‑by‑lap position data
SELECT DISTINCT "race_id"
FROM   "lap_positions"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Raw lap‑position rows for the first five laps of one sample race (id = 1)
SELECT *
FROM   "lap_positions"
WHERE  "race_id" = 1
  AND  "lap" BETWEEN 1 AND 5
  AND  "lap_type" = 'Race'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Show the starting grid (lap = 0) for that race
SELECT "driver_id", "position" AS "grid_position"
FROM   "lap_positions"
WHERE  "race_id" = 1
  AND  "lap" = 0
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) All pit‑stops that happened in laps 1‑5
SELECT DISTINCT "race_id", "driver_id", "lap", "duration"
FROM   "pit_stops"
WHERE  "lap" BETWEEN 1 AND 5
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) All retirements that happened in laps ≤ 5
SELECT DISTINCT "race_id", "driver_id", "lap", "status_id", "retirement_type"
FROM   "retirements"
WHERE  "lap" <= 5
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Compare grid vs. lap‑1 position to flag start‑related gains/losses
SELECT g."driver_id",
       g."position"      AS "grid_pos",
       l1."position"     AS "lap1_pos",
       (g."position" - l1."position") AS "delta"
FROM   "lap_positions" AS g
JOIN   "lap_positions" AS l1
       ON  l1."race_id"  = g."race_id"
       AND l1."driver_id"= g."driver_id"
       AND l1."lap"      = 1
       AND l1."lap_type" = 'Race'
WHERE  g."race_id" = 1
  AND  g."lap"   = 0
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Position order by driver for every lap 0‑5 (handy to derive overtakes)
SELECT "driver_id", "lap", "position"
FROM   "lap_positions"
WHERE  "race_id" = 1
  AND  "lap" BETWEEN 0 AND 5
  AND  "lap_type" = 'Race'
ORDER BY "driver_id", "lap"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Identify laps where a driver pitted AND lost positions in that same lap
SELECT lp."driver_id",
       lp."lap",
       lp."position"          AS "pos_before",
       lp2."position"         AS "pos_after",
       (lp2."position" - lp."position") AS "lost_places"
FROM   "lap_positions" AS lp
JOIN   "lap_positions" AS lp2
       ON lp2."race_id"   = lp."race_id"
      AND lp2."driver_id" = lp."driver_id"
      AND lp2."lap"       = lp."lap" + 1            -- next lap
JOIN   "pit_stops"  AS ps
       ON ps."race_id"    = lp."race_id"
      AND ps."driver_id"  = lp."driver_id"
      AND ps."lap"        = lp."lap"
WHERE  lp."race_id" = 1
  AND  lp."lap" BETWEEN 1 AND 5
  AND  lp."lap_type" = 'Race'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Drivers who retired in laps ≤ 5 and their last recorded position
SELECT lp."driver_id",
       MAX(lp."lap")                 AS "last_scored_lap",
       MAX(lp."position")            AS "position_when_retired"
FROM   "lap_positions" AS lp
JOIN   "retirements"  AS r
       ON r."race_id"   = lp."race_id"
      AND r."driver_id" = lp."driver_id"
WHERE  lp."race_id" = 1
  AND  lp."lap" BETWEEN 1 AND 5
GROUP BY lp."driver_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10) (Template) Categorise every over‑take event in laps 1‑5
--     This is only a sketch – you would adapt it into a CTE or series of CTEs
--     to finally COUNT(*) for each category.
WITH pos AS (
  SELECT "race_id", "driver_id", "lap", "position",
         LAG("position") OVER (PARTITION BY "driver_id" ORDER BY "lap") AS "prev_pos"
  FROM   "lap_positions"
  WHERE  "race_id" = 1
    AND  "lap_type" = 'Race'
    AND  "lap" BETWEEN 0 AND 5
),
moves AS (
  SELECT *, COALESCE("prev_pos", "position") AS "prev_pos_filled",
         ("prev_pos" - "position")            AS "delta"   -- positive = gained places
  FROM   pos
  WHERE  "lap" BETWEEN 1 AND 5
),
tagged AS (
  SELECT m.*,
         CASE
           WHEN EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM "retirements" r
                        WHERE r."race_id"  = m."race_id"
                          AND r."driver_id"= m."driver_id"
                          AND r."lap"      = m."lap")        THEN 'retirement'
           WHEN EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM "pit_stops" p
                        WHERE p."race_id"  = m."race_id"
                          AND p."driver_id"= m."driver_id"
                          AND p."lap"      = m."lap")        THEN 'pit_stop'
           WHEN (m."lap" = 1)                                 THEN 'start_related'
           ELSE 'on_track'
         END AS "overtake_type"
  FROM   moves AS m
  WHERE  "delta" <> 0           -- an actual change
)
SELECT "overtake_type", COUNT(*) AS "num_overtakes"
FROM   tagged
GROUP  BY "overtake_type"
LIMIT 20;
```
The tenth query is essentially the “answer query” (one race example). You would  

• Remove the LIMIT 20,  
• Replace `= 1` with the race you are studying (or a variable/parameter), and  
• Possibly refine the CASE logic (e.g. checking whether the position change happened because another driver retired, rather than the retiring driver himself).  

Running it will yield the requested count of overtakes in the first five laps broken down into  
retirement‑caused, pit‑stop‑caused, start‑related, and standard on‑track passes.
